========================================================================================================
COMPARABILITY COST OF bf16-8bit-gc-refcpu AGAINST THE FROZEN fp32 FULL-FINETUNE 1.5B/3B RESULTS
========================================================================================================
  EXACT (comparability cost ZERO, price is time only):
    gradient checkpointing (recompute; identical gradients)
    reference offload to CPU (same weights, same KL, different device)
  NUMERICS (not exact; ONE control tests them together):
    pure bfloat16 weights/grads (fp32 -> bfloat16)
    optimizer adamw_bnb_8bit (quantized moments)
  GEOMETRY (not comparable without matched controls at every scale):
    (none)

  REQUIRED CONTROLS: 3 runs -- re-run ONE frozen 1.5B cell (3 seeds) under this
    stack and check the event dating does not move. 5.3 h A100 = $3.63 spot, AT 400 STEPS/RUN.
    Full-finetune geometry is preserved, so no matched LoRA grid is owed.

  For contrast: the LoRA path would owe 10x more control compute (55.1 h vs 5.3 h at 400 steps/run).
  This is the argument for the boring stack. LoRA is the cheaper RUN and by far the
  more expensive RESULT.
========================================================================================================
================================================================================================
CHECKPOINT PLAN  (7.62B params, 400 steps @ 77.1 s/step = 8.56 h, backend=hub @ 150 MB/s)
================================================================================================
  one resumable checkpoint = weights 15.2 GB + optimizer 15.2 GB = 30.5 GB  (4.0 B/param, bfloat16/adamw_bnb_8bit)
    weights-only would be 15.2 GB, but resuming from it resets Adam's moments to zero,
    which is a different run. The 30.5 GB figure is the one that can be resumed.
  write cost   30.5 GB / 150 MB/s = 203 s per checkpoint
  Young/Daly   tau* = sqrt(2*C*M) = sqrt(2*203*43200) = 4190 s = 54 steps  (M = 12 h MTBE)

  cadence         ckpts   GB written   write time   overhead   E[wasted]   verdict
  every 5    steps    80       2438      16253 s        53%      0.03 h   COSTS MORE THAN IT SAVES  <- naive
  every 25   steps    16        488       3251 s        11%      0.14 h   net gain
  every 50   steps     8        244       1625 s         5%      0.27 h   net gain
  every 100  steps     4        122        813 s         3%      0.55 h   net gain  <- CHOSEN

  CHOSEN: save_steps=100, save_total_limit=2 -> 4 checkpoints, 122 GB written, 813 s (2.6% overhead)
  RISK:   P(evicted during a 8.56 h run at 12 h MTBE) = 51%.  E[compute lost] 1.93 h unprotected -> 0.57 h protected.
  NET:    checkpointing pays for itself  (+0.77 USD at $0.68/h spot, checkpoint write time included)
  SCOPE:  this verdict prices EVICTION INSURANCE ONLY. A checkpoint is also a scientific artifact --
          scripts/sweep_source_c.py exists to sweep saved checkpoints and cannot run because none were
          ever kept. That is a SEPARATE justification with a different cadence (driven by the
          resolution you want on the layer/step grid, not by MTBE), and it must be argued on its own
          rather than smuggled in here. Note also that it is NOT needed for the Source C trajectory:
          PrimeProbeCallback probes the LIVE model in-run, so the trajectory needs no saved weights.
  NOTE:   MTBE is the one number here that is a GUESS. It is region-, SKU- and hour-dependent and Azure
          publishes no SLA for it. Re-run plan(mtbf_h=...) at 3 h and 48 h before trusting any verdict
          near the boundary; the cadence moves as sqrt(M), so it is robust, but the worth-it verdict is not.
